<script>
  import Aside from '$lib/components/Aside.svelte';
  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

In the summer of 2024, reporters at Gothamist published ["NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year"](https://gothamist.com/news/hot-spots-nypd-data-shows-most-shootings-occur-on-the-same-blocks-year-after-year), a data-driven investigation into gun violence in New York City.

The analysis, by reporters Brittany Kriegstein and Jaclyn Jeffrey-Wilensky, showed that shooting incidents were not, as some might assume, a widespread problem that reached every corner of the city.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/gothamist-story.png"
  alt="Gothamist's 'Hot spots: NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year' story page"
  chromeTitle="Hot spots: NYPD data shows most shootings occur on the same blocks, year after year"
  chromeUrl="https://gothamist.com/news/hot-spots-nypd-data-shows-most-shootings-occur-on-the-same-blocks-year-after-year"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Instead, by downloading and mapping the data published to [the city's open data portal](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Public-Safety/NYPD-Shooting-Incident-Data-Historic-/833y-fsy8/data_preview), they found that gunfire was concentrated in a small number of areas, with nine areas accounting for a massive share of shootings.

"Just 4% of New York City’s 120,000 blocks account for nearly all of the city's shootings," the story reported. "Life even two or three blocks away from these nine hot spots can be dramatically different, with less violence, fewer crimes and residents who say they feel relatively safe."

Alongside the story, Gothamist published [an interactive map](https://gothamist.com/news/new-york-city-gun-violence-map) that allowed readers to see their findings and explore the data for themselves. It shaded every Census block in the five boroughs by how many shooting incidents the NYPD recorded there over the previous four years.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/gothamist-gun-violence.png"
  alt="Gothamist's 'Map: Where New York City's shootings happen' showing a map of the five boroughs shaded by census block, with the darkest blocks concentrated in parts of the Bronx, Brooklyn, and Harlem"
  chromeTitle="Map: Where New York City's shootings happen"
  chromeUrl="https://gothamist.com/news/new-york-city-gun-violence-map"
/>

Their work follows a common model for data journalism: Mapping a dataset to reveal patterns and steer reporting, then publishing an interactive version to let readers place themselves in the story, regardless of where they live.

Today you are going to learn how to do just that. We'll cover how to plot points, shade areas and invite users to interact with the data.

Like Gothamist, our source will be the New York City data portal. But instead of crime, we'll be mapping the city's massive population of trees.

## Part 1: Two lists of trees

In 2015, NYC Parks ran its third decadal street tree census — a citizen science effort called [TreesCount!](https://www.nycgovparks.org/trees/treescount). Over 2,200 volunteers walked every block in the five boroughs with tablets, identifying every street tree they found and recording its species, trunk diameter, health, and exact location. They found 666,134 trees of 132 different species.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/treescount-homepage.png"
  alt="The NYC Parks TreesCount! 2015 homepage showing data visualizations about New York's street trees"
  chromeTitle="TreesCount! 2015"
  chromeUrl="https://www.nycgovparks.org/trees/treescount"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That dataset is a goldmine of potential journalism, but today it's only half our subject. The other half is a much shorter list from the same department — compiled decades earlier and updated for the first time in October 2024.

The first story is **the Great Trees of New York City**. In 1985, NYC Parks put out a call for nominations: send us the trees in your neighborhood that matter. Volunteers and arborists from every borough wrote in with their candidates — trees of unusual size, rare species, strange form, or deep historical association. The department chose 120 of them and called them the Great Trees. Nearly forty years later, Parks ran the search again and updated the list: 59 of the original trees were still standing, and the committee added 61 new ones from 270 public nominations. The full roster today is 120 named, story-rich trees across the five boroughs. It includes the Camperdown elm south of the Prospect Park Boathouse, a tree Marianne Moore wrote a poem to help save in the 1960s. It includes the two American beeches at Maple Grove Cemetery in Queens that shade the grave of Jacob Riis's wife. And it includes an American sycamore behind the Conference House at the southern tip of Staten Island, the colonial-era building where British envoys met with John Adams and Ben Franklin in 1776. Each one is a small piece of New York history, kept alive because someone noticed.

The second story is **the London planetree**, and for this one we go back to the census. That's the tree Robert Moses, the unelected power broker who ran NYC Parks from 1934 to 1960, planted everywhere he built. Along his parkways. In his parks. Up and down street after street. Moses's favorite tree is still the most common street tree in New York — about one in ten of every street tree you pass is a London planetree, a direct artifact of one man's taste imposed on the city seventy years ago. The leaf in the NYC Parks logo is widely believed to depict one.

Two very different trees, two very different stories. And we are going to put them both on the same page.

### The pedagogical shape of today

The Great Trees story uses a small, curated dataset — 120 individual trees, each with a story attached. We can plot every one of them as a dot on the map without the computer breaking a sweat, and give every dot a popup the reader will actually want to click.

The planetree story is different. There are something like 70,000 London planetrees in the census, and plotting each one as an individual dot would produce a file too large to ship cheaply to a reader's browser. For data at that scale we need to summarize — to aggregate each neighborhood's planetrees into a single count, and shade the map accordingly.

This is a real tradeoff every news graphics desk makes. Point maps are expressive and personal — readers can find their own block, their own tree. Aggregated maps are efficient and reveal patterns — readers see the shape of the whole city. Neither is better. They answer different questions.

Today you're going to build both, on the same page, so you can feel the difference.

### Set up your project

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `nyc-trees`. Clone it, open it in Visual Studio Code, and install the dependencies you know so well:

```bash
npm install
```

Then install one more package we need this week — [MapLibre GL JS](https://maplibre.org/), the open-source mapping library that powers our map:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl
```

This is the first time we've added a dependency beyond what the template ships with. Before, `npm install` was pulling down whatever the template's `package.json` declared. Now we are asking npm to add something new to that list. If you open `package.json` you should see `maplibre-gl` there now. When a classmate clones your repository later and runs `npm install`, they'll get MapLibre automatically.

Now grab the data. I've posted two files in the class chat: `great-trees.json` and `planetrees-by-nta.json`. Create a `src/lib/data/` folder in your project and drop both files in there. GeoJSON is valid JSON, so we're saving them with a `.json` extension to let Vite import them directly.

Start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

And open [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) in your browser.

## Part 2: Your first map

The class template already ships with the components we need: `Map`, `MapLayer`, and `Geocoder`. You'll find them in `src/lib/components/`. Open `Map.svelte` and read the comment block at the top. It explains what the component does and how to use it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/vscode-map-component.png"
  alt="VS Code showing the Map.svelte component's opening comment block with its USAGE EXAMPLE"
  showChrome={false}
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

`Map` is a wrapper around MapLibre. It handles the fiddly parts — creating the map, loading the basemap tiles, re-initializing on theme changes, cleaning up on unmount — so you don't have to. All you need to give it is a center, a zoom level, and an optional theme. It will render whatever you put inside it as a layer on top of the base map.

Let's see it in action.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace the contents with:

```svelte
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
</script>

<h1>The trees of New York</h1>

<Map
  longitude={-73.968}
  latitude={40.785}
  zoom={12}
  theme="positron"
  caption="Central Park and a chunk of Manhattan, waiting for trees."
  credit="OpenFreeMap / OpenStreetMap contributors"
/>
```

Save the file and check your browser. You should see a clean pale-gray map of Manhattan with a caption underneath.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/bare-map.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of Manhattan with no data overlaid, sitting below an H1 reading 'The trees of New York'"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Play with it a little. Drag the map around. Zoom in and out with the scroll wheel. Then try changing the `theme` prop to `"liberty"` or `"bright"` and save — the basemap should swap without you reloading the page.

Change `longitude`, `latitude`, and `zoom` and watch the map fly to the new location. These aren't just initial values; they are live, reactive props. Remember everything you already know about `$state` and reactive props from weeks past. That knowledge now controls a map.

## Part 3: Add a geocoder

A map that starts centered on Midtown is nice, but a reader doesn't live in Midtown. They live in Bed-Stuy, or Flushing, or Mott Haven, or upstate somewhere and they're homesick. Let's give them a way to point the map at the place they care about.

The template includes a third component called `Geocoder`. It's a search box that translates a typed address into geographic coordinates using the free [Nominatim](https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/) service, the open-source alternative to Google Places. When the user picks a result, it fires an `onresult` callback with the coordinates.

Let's wire it up.

In `+page.svelte`, add the import and three reactive state variables for the coordinates and zoom:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3,6-8"}
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
  import Geocoder from '$lib/components/Geocoder.svelte';

  // Start centered on the middle of Manhattan.
  let longitude = $state(-73.968);
  let latitude = $state(40.785);
  let zoom = $state(12);
</script>
```

Now replace the hard-coded numbers in the `<Map>` tag with the state variables, and add a `<Geocoder>` above the map with a callback that updates them.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3-10,14-16"}
<h1>The trees of New York</h1>

<Geocoder
  label="Find your neighborhood"
  placeholder="Enter an address in New York…"
  onresult={(result) => {
    longitude = result.lng;
    latitude = result.lat;
    zoom = 15;
  }}
/>

<Map
  {longitude}
  {latitude}
  {zoom}
  theme="positron"
  caption="Central Park and a chunk of Manhattan, waiting for trees."
/>
```

Notice the shorthand `{longitude}` — that's the same as writing `longitude={longitude}`. Svelte lets you skip the repetition when the prop name matches the variable name. We've been using this pattern since week three.

Save and try it. Type your home address into the search box, pick a result from the dropdown, and watch the map glide over to your block. The animated transition is built into MapLibre — any time those props change, the underlying map calls `flyTo` with a smooth easing curve.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/geocoder-in-action.png"
  alt="The page showing a search box with the query 'Newmark Graduate School of Journalism', a dropdown of matching results, and a map that has flown to Midtown Manhattan"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

This is the reactive pattern you know, now connected to cartography. The Geocoder updates three state variables. Those variables flow into the Map as props. The Map reacts to the change by animating to the new location. No imperative code, no manual event wiring. You describe what should be true, and Svelte and MapLibre work out how to get there.

Try searching for a few addresses outside Manhattan. Search for your childhood home. Search for a place you've always been curious about. This kind of personal, place-based interaction is one of the most engaging tools a journalist has. The reader enters the story through their own front door.

## Part 4: Add the Great Tree points

So far our map is empty. Let's change that. We'll start with the smaller of our two datasets — the Great Trees — and plot every one of them as an individual point.

### Load the data

Because the data file lives inside your project, you can import it directly. Vite will bake it into the build as a plain JavaScript object — no network request, no loading state, no `+page.js`. The data is just *there* when the page renders.

Update the `<script>` block in `+page.svelte` to import the file:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4"}
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
  import Geocoder from '$lib/components/Geocoder.svelte';
  import greatTrees from '$lib/data/great-trees.json';

  let longitude = $state(-73.968);
  let latitude = $state(40.785);
  let zoom = $state(12);
</script>
```

Before we plot anything, let's confirm the data is really there. Add a `console.log` at the top of the script block:

```js
console.log('great trees:', greatTrees.features.length, 'points');
```

Save, open your browser's developer console (Cmd+Option+J on Mac, Ctrl+Shift+J on Windows), and refresh. You should see:

```
great trees: 119 points
```

NYC Parks lists 120 Great Trees on their site; the scraper that pulled our file missed one. This is normal. Scraping almost always leaks a record or two, and it's worth knowing about your own data's gaps before a reader finds them for you.

### Plot the points

Time to plant some trees. The template's `MapLayer` component takes a GeoJSON collection and a MapLibre paint object and renders the data on the parent map. Place it as a child of `<Map>`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2,10-19"}
<script>
  import MapLayer from '$lib/components/MapLayer.svelte';
  // ...existing imports and state...
</script>

<!-- Geocoder -->

<Map {longitude} {latitude} {zoom} theme="positron" caption="The Great Trees of New York City, named by NYC Parks for their historical, botanical, and cultural significance.">
  <MapLayer
    id="great-trees"
    type="circle"
    data={greatTrees}
    paint={{
      'circle-color': '#d4a017',
      'circle-radius': 5,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#3a2818',
      'circle-stroke-width': 1,
      'circle-opacity': 0.9
    }}
  />
</Map>
```

Save. Your map should now be studded with amber dots.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/great-tree-dots.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of the five boroughs studded with amber dots, clustered in parks and cemeteries but scattered everywhere from Van Cortlandt Park in the Bronx down through Central Park and Prospect Park to Tottenville on Staten Island"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Zoom out and look at how the dots distribute across the city. The big parks each hold a handful — Central Park, Prospect Park, Van Cortlandt, Forest Park. But many are not in parks at all. You'll find dots in church graveyards, inside historic cemeteries, in the courtyards of old houses, and on stretches of regular sidewalk where a neighborhood rallied to keep one giant tree alive through decades of street work.

The density itself tells a story. There's no "Great Trees district" — they're sprinkled where New Yorkers happened to nominate them. A block in Mapes Park in the Bronx has one because residents fought to protect an American Elm during park renovations. A corner of Maple Grove Cemetery in Queens has two because a local historian pointed out they shade the grave of Jacob Riis's wife. Each dot is an act of attention, not a statistical sample.

This is exactly the kind of dataset a point map was built for. Every record is a named, locatable, story-bearing thing. The count is small enough that MapLibre can render all of them instantly. And because every tree has a narrative attached, every dot is an invitation for the reader to click — which is what we'll build next.

### Add a popup

Readers always want to click things. Let's reward that instinct. Each of the Great Trees in our file arrives with its species and a precise description of where to find it. About half also come with a photograph of the tree itself. And from the tree's ID we can construct a link back to the full entry on NYC Parks.

`MapLayer` accepts a `popup` prop: a function that takes a clicked feature and returns an HTML string. MapLayer handles the rest — positioning the popup, wiring the click handler, changing the cursor on hover.

Add a `popup` function to your `MapLayer`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="10-22"}
<MapLayer
  id="great-trees"
  type="circle"
  data={greatTrees}
  paint={{
    'circle-color': '#d4a017',
    'circle-radius': 5,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#3a2818',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-opacity': 0.9
  }}
  popup={(feature) => {
    const p = feature.properties;
    const parksUrl = `https://www.nycgovparks.org/facilities/great-trees/${p.id}`;
    return `
      <strong>${p.species || 'Great Tree'}</strong>
      <p style="max-width: 240px; margin: 0.25em 0;">${p.description}</p>
      ${p.image ? `<img src="${p.image}" style="max-width: 240px; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0.5em 0;">` : ''}
      <a href="${parksUrl}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read on NYC Parks →</a>
    `;
  }}
/>
```

Save. Click one of the amber dots. A popup should appear with the tree's species, its location, a photo if one exists, and a link back to the full NYC Parks page.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/great-tree-popup.png"
  alt="A zoomed-in map showing an amber dot over Prospect Park with a popup reading 'Camperdown elm' above a line about its location south of the Boathouse, a photo of the sprawling tree, and a link back to NYC Parks"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

A few things worth noticing about the `popup` prop.

It's a *function*, not a string. That's the key to making popups flexible. You pass in a template function, and MapLayer calls it every time a tree is clicked. Because the function has access to the clicked feature's full properties object, you can construct whatever HTML makes sense for the data at hand.

It returns ordinary HTML as a string. That's why we use backtick [template literals](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals) here — they let us interpolate variables right into the markup with the `${}` syntax. You can put inline styling, nested markup, and outbound links in here. Notice the inline `max-width` and `height: auto` on the photo tag to keep it from overflowing the popup or squishing its aspect ratio.

Notice also that we are not storing the NYC Parks URL in our data file. We construct it from the tree's `id` with another template literal one line up. This is a small but powerful habit: when a value can be derived, don't store it. The file stays smaller, the data stays honest, and if NYC Parks ever restructures their URLs we only have one line to change.

And it gracefully handles missing data. About half of the trees don't have a photo in the scraped file. The ternary `? :` guard means the `<img>` tag only appears when `p.image` is a real string. The reader never sees a broken image icon, and we never ship an `<img src="null">` to the page. This kind of defensive formatting separates professional work from a prototype.

That's all the point-map form has to teach us. A small curated list, one MapLayer, one popup template. Clean and direct. Now let's see what happens when the dataset gets bigger.

## Part 5: Swap in the planetree choropleth

The Great Trees were easy: a tiny handful of points, nowhere near a megabyte, no trouble at all for MapLibre to render.

The London planetree is a different animal. There are around 70,000 of them in the 2015 census. If we tried to plot every one as an individual dot:

- The file that ships to each reader's browser would be roughly 8 to 10 megabytes. On classroom Wi-Fi that's a two-second wait; on a fading cell connection at the back of a bus, it's a spinner the reader gives up on.
- The browser would need to draw 70,000 shapes at once, which is doable but starts to stutter on older devices.
- Most of those dots would overlap with their neighbors anyway, so the visual payoff would be a blobby green haze — not the thing a reader can click or parse.

When a dataset is too big to ship as individual points, you have a few options. You could sample — show only every tenth tree. You could cluster — let MapLibre group nearby trees into expandable dots. Or you could **aggregate**: summarize the data into a smaller unit of geography before it ever leaves your machine.

Gothamist aggregated their shootings data to census blocks. We're going to aggregate our planetree data to a different administrative unit: the **Neighborhood Tabulation Area**, or NTA. NYC's Department of City Planning created NTAs to approximate the neighborhoods most New Yorkers recognize — "Park Slope-Gowanus," "Astoria-Ditmars-Steinway," "Washington Heights North" — by grouping census tracts into medium-sized statistical geographies. There are 195 of them, compared to 666,000 trees. The compression ratio is immense.

The `planetrees-by-nta.geojson` file I posted in the class chat is the result of that aggregation. Every one of the 195 NTA polygons arrives with a London planetree count already baked into its properties — along with richer context we'll get to in a moment.

### Load the second file

Add a second import in `+page.svelte` alongside the Great Trees:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
import greatTrees from '$lib/data/great-trees.json';
import planetrees from '$lib/data/planetrees-by-nta.json';
```

Pop open your browser console and inspect one of the NTA features:

```js
console.log(planetrees.features[0].properties);
```

You should see a property bag that looks roughly like this:

```js
{
  ntacode: "BK09",
  ntaname: "Brooklyn Heights-Cobble Hill",
  boroname: "Brooklyn",
  total_all_species: 5431,
  top_species: [
    { name: "honeylocust", count: 892 },
    { name: "London planetree", count: 734 },
    { name: "pin oak", count: 612 },
    { name: "Callery pear", count: 498 },
    { name: "littleleaf linden", count: 389 }
  ],
  count_total: 734,
  count_good: 521,
  count_fair: 176,
  count_poor: 37,
  count_small: 42,
  count_medium: 312,
  count_large: 380,
  rank: 2
}
```

Pause and absorb what's in there. Each NTA carries not just a single London planetree count but a whole dossier: how many total street trees it has of all species, the five most common species in rank order, and several pre-computed slices of its planetree population by health and by trunk size. The `rank` field is especially interesting — it's where the London planetree sits in the species leaderboard of this NTA. A `1` means Moses's tree wins here. A `2` or `3` means something else is in front.

This is what it looks like to pre-compute your story. A raw 70,000-tree dataset got squeezed into 195 feature-rich polygons before it ever touched the wire.

Now you can see the contrast between our two data files. Roughly a hundred and twenty points on one side. Fewer than two hundred polygons on the other. Both describe the same city, but in completely different ways. That shape difference is what drives the swap we're about to make.

### Swap the layer

Remove the Great Tree `MapLayer` from inside your `<Map>` block, and delete the matching `import greatTrees` line at the top of the script. Don't worry — it's not gone forever. We'll talk about how to bring it back at the end of class.

In its place, add two new `MapLayer`s: one that fills each NTA with a color based on its London planetree count, and one that draws the outlines between them.

A [choropleth map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choropleth_map) shades each region of a map by a numeric value. We're going to shade each NTA by its `count_total` — the number of London planetrees living inside it.

```svelte
<MapLayer
  id="nta-fill"
  type="fill"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'fill-color': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'count_total'],
      0, '#f0f7f4',
      250, '#95d5b2',
      1000, '#40916c',
      3000, '#1b4332'
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.7
  }}
/>
<MapLayer
  id="nta-outline"
  type="line"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'line-color': '#1b4332',
    'line-width': 0.5
  }}
/>
```

Reading the fill-color expression out loud: *interpolate linearly by the `count_total` property of each polygon. At 0 trees, use nearly-white. At 250 trees, a light green. At 1000, a medium green. At 3000 or more, a deep forest green.* MapLibre smooths between the stops automatically.

Also update the `Map`'s caption, since what it's showing has completely changed:

```svelte
<Map {longitude} {latitude} {zoom} theme="positron" caption="Every one of NYC's 70,000-plus London planetrees from the 2015 street tree census, aggregated to the neighborhood.">
```

Save. The amber dots are gone, and the city now fills with varying shades of green.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/choropleth.png"
  alt="A map of NYC with neighborhoods shaded from pale green to dark forest green by London planetree count. The darkest polygons concentrate along the Upper East Side, through brownstone Brooklyn, and out along the old parkways of Queens"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

You're now looking at Moses's New York. The darkest polygons are the NTAs where his tree is thickest on the ground — along the Upper East Side, through brownstone Brooklyn, ribboning out into Queens along the old parkways. The lightest polygons are the places his planting campaign never fully reached: the outermost edges of the city, the industrial corners, some of the newer developments.

### Add a popup

Let's make those polygons clickable. Add a `popup` to the fill layer that tells the reader what they're looking at:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="11-24"}
<MapLayer
  id="nta-fill"
  type="fill"
  data={planetrees}
  paint={{
    'fill-color': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'count_total'],
      0, '#f0f7f4', 250, '#95d5b2',
      1000, '#40916c', 3000, '#1b4332'
    ],
    'fill-opacity': 0.7
  }}
  popup={(feature) => {
    const p = feature.properties;
    const rankLine = p.rank === 1
      ? `<strong>#1 species in this neighborhood</strong>`
      : `London planetree ranks #${p.rank} — top species is ${p.top_species[0].name} (${p.top_species[0].count.toLocaleString()})`;
    return `
      <strong>${p.ntaname}</strong><br>
      ${p.count_total.toLocaleString()} London planetrees<br>
      ${rankLine}<br>
      <em>${p.count_good.toLocaleString()} Good · ${p.count_fair.toLocaleString()} Fair · ${p.count_poor.toLocaleString()} Poor</em>
    `;
  }}
/>
```

Save and click around. Each NTA now tells you three things: how many London planetrees it holds, where that tree ranks among all species growing there, and how those planetrees are faring healthwise.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/nta-popup.png"
  alt="A popup over a Brooklyn NTA reading 'Bedford-Stuyvesant (West): 1,847 London planetrees, London planetree ranks #2 — top species is honeylocust (2,104), and a health breakdown of 1,312 Good · 421 Fair · 114 Poor'"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That `rank` field is where the story gets interesting. Flick around the map and find the NTAs where Moses's tree is *not* #1. Some of the outer-borough neighborhoods — parts of Staten Island, corners of Eastern Queens — show honeylocust or pin oak as the champion instead, with the planetree trailing at #3 or #4. You are looking at the limits of one man's reach.

### Let the reader switch slices

Our choropleth colors every NTA by its total London planetree count. But the data file has other slices baked in — `count_good`, `count_fair`, `count_poor`, plus three buckets by trunk size. Each slice tells a different story.

Let's let the reader switch between them.

Add a state variable for the active slice and a dropdown that sets it:

```svelte
<script>
  // ...existing imports and state...
  let colorBy = $state('count_total');
</script>

<!-- Somewhere above the map: -->
<label>
  Color neighborhoods by:
  <select bind:value={colorBy}>
    <option value="count_total">Total London planetrees</option>
    <option value="count_good">Good-health only</option>
    <option value="count_fair">Fair-health only</option>
    <option value="count_poor">Poor-health only</option>
    <option value="count_large">Large trunks (30″+)</option>
    <option value="count_medium">Medium trunks (10–29″)</option>
    <option value="count_small">Small trunks (under 10″)</option>
  </select>
</label>
```

Now derive a `paint` object that references the selected property:

```js
let fillPaint = $derived({
  'fill-color': [
    'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', colorBy],
    0, '#f0f7f4',
    50, '#95d5b2',
    250, '#40916c',
    1000, '#1b4332'
  ],
  'fill-opacity': 0.7
});
```

And pass that derived paint object to the fill layer instead of the inline version:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4"}
<MapLayer
  id="nta-fill"
  type="fill"
  paint={fillPaint}
  data={planetrees}
  popup={/* same as before */}
/>
```

Save and try the dropdown. Switch to "Poor-health only" and watch the map re-color around the neighborhoods carrying the highest count of struggling planetrees — which, in theory, could correlate with heat, soil compaction, or neglect. Switch to "Large trunks" and you'll see the NTAs where Moses's original mid-century plantings still dominate, versus places where the canopy has been turned over.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/color-by-large.png"
  alt="The map re-colored to show only London planetrees with trunk diameters of 30 inches or more, revealing Moses's mid-century planting corridors concentrated along Riverside Drive, Ocean Parkway, and the grand boulevards of the Bronx"
  chromeTitle="The trees of New York"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Take a moment to notice what just happened. We changed **one reactive variable** — `colorBy` — and the map re-colored itself across 195 polygons. No imperative redraw calls, no manual event listeners. Svelte saw that the `paint` prop of the `MapLayer` depended on `colorBy`, and `MapLayer` saw that its own paint had changed, and MapLibre saw that the layer's style had changed. Every piece of the stack held up its end of the reactive contract.

Worth noting what you *cannot* do with this approach, because the tradeoff matters editorially. The choropleth can only show slices that were pre-computed into the data file. We could not, at runtime, ask "shade by poor-health planetrees that are also large-trunked." We'd have to go back to the Python that produced this file, add a `count_poor_large` property, regenerate, and redeploy. For many graphics projects, that's fine — the fixed menu is the whole story. For others you'd keep the raw data and aggregate in the browser. Always worth knowing which side of that line you're on before you start.

## Part 6: Editorial framing and sources

Two maps on one page, and nothing yet explains what the reader is looking at or where any of it came from. Let's fix that.

Replace the bare `<h1>` with a proper framing, and add a source credit at the bottom:

```svelte
<h1>The trees of New York</h1>

<p>
  In 1985, NYC Parks asked New Yorkers to nominate the trees in their
  neighborhoods that mattered. The agency returned to the list in
  October 2024 and named 120 of them — the Great Trees of New York.
  Meanwhile, the most common tree on the city's streets remains the
  London planetree, planted by the thousands decades earlier under
  Parks Commissioner Robert Moses. One list is stories; the other is
  scale. Both still shape the canopy of the five boroughs. Find yours
  on the map.
</p>

<!-- Geocoder, filters, and map go here -->

<footer>
  <p>
    Sources: NYC Parks'
    <a href="https://www.nycgovparks.org/facilities/great-trees" target="_blank">Great Trees of New York City</a>
    list, updated October 2024; and the NYC Parks
    <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Environment/2015-Street-Tree-Census-Tree-Data/uvpi-gqnh" target="_blank">2015 Street Tree Census</a>,
    via NYC Open Data.
    Neighborhood boundaries from the
    <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/City-Government/2010-Neighborhood-Tabulation-Areas-NTAs-/cpf4-rkhq" target="_blank">2010 NTA dataset</a>.
    Address geocoding by
    <a href="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/" target="_blank">Nominatim</a>.
    Basemap tiles by <a href="https://openfreemap.org/" target="_blank">OpenFreeMap</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 2015 census is the most recent complete inventory of New York's
    street trees. Some have been removed or replaced since, and new trees
    have been planted. This map is a snapshot of what volunteers recorded
    a decade ago, not a live registry. Species counts per neighborhood
    were pre-computed from the raw census; filters showing different
    slices rely on those pre-computed values, not on ad-hoc queries
    against the full dataset.
  </p>
</footer>
```

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own two-view map

Find a point dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/) or another public source that includes latitude and longitude. Build a page with two map views of it: a point map for the raw records, and an aggregated view for a broader pattern.

Your project must have:

- A `Map` component with data plotted from your chosen dataset
- A `Geocoder` for navigation
- At least one point-data `MapLayer` with a `popup` template
- At least one aggregated `MapLayer` (you may pre-aggregate in Python, or aggregate in the browser using `$derived.by`)
- An interactive control (dropdown, checkbox, or slider) that changes what the map shows
- An editorial frame: a headline, an introductory paragraph, and a source credit with a methodology caveat

Some datasets that could work well:

- NYC Film Permits (aggregate by neighborhood)
- Motor Vehicle Crashes (points for individual incidents, choropleth for totals)
- 311 Noise Complaints from the past month (points for specific complaints, choropleth for volume)
- Restaurant Inspections (points for individual restaurants, choropleth for grade distribution)
- A different tree species from the same census (Callery pear has an invasive-tree angle; ginkgo has the stinky-female-tree angle)

Deploy to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to walk the class through your map and explain:

- The dataset you chose and what story the geography tells
- Why you chose to aggregate what you aggregated, and what the tradeoffs were
- At least one editorial decision you made about what to show and what to leave out — and why
- Anything you got from an AI assistant that you had to modify or rewrite yourself

### Task 3: Look at it on your phone

Before you present, open your live site on your phone. Does the map fill the screen? Do the controls stack cleanly? Can you tap a polygon with your thumb?

Most readers of most interactive news maps will encounter them on a phone. A map that only works on a laptop is a map that fails most of its readers. Fix anything that isn't working before next class.
